1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,279
Almost everything you own crossed an ocean

2
00:00:03,279 --> 00:00:04,451
in a steel box,

3
00:00:04,451 --> 00:00:06,950
and every one of those boxes was

4
00:00:06,950 --> 00:00:09,137
lifted by a crane like this.

5
00:00:09,317 --> 00:00:12,714
A big ship-to-shore crane can weigh around

6
00:00:12,714 --> 00:00:14,170
two thousand tons,

7
00:00:14,170 --> 00:00:17,891
stand taller than a thirty-story building with

8
00:00:17,891 --> 00:00:19,104
its arm raised,

9
00:00:19,104 --> 00:00:21,531
and lift a box every couple of

10
00:00:21,531 --> 00:00:24,038
minutes, all day and all night.

11
00:00:24,218 --> 00:00:27,181
Today, ten parts of a port and

12
00:00:27,181 --> 00:00:28,860
its giant cranes,

13
00:00:28,860 --> 00:00:31,230
and why each one exists.

14
00:00:31,580 --> 00:00:33,259
Before the container,

15
00:00:33,259 --> 00:00:35,976
loading a ship was slow and brutal

16
00:00:35,976 --> 00:00:36,375
work.

17
00:00:36,555 --> 00:00:38,763
Dock workers carried sacks,

18
00:00:38,763 --> 00:00:41,216
barrels and crates one by one,

19
00:00:41,216 --> 00:00:43,587
and a ship could spend a week

20
00:00:43,587 --> 00:00:44,241
in port.

21
00:00:44,421 --> 00:00:46,812
Then, in nineteen fifty-six,

22
00:00:46,812 --> 00:00:51,251
an American trucking businessman named Malcom McLean

23
00:00:51,251 --> 00:00:55,007
sent fifty-eight metal boxes from New Jersey

24
00:00:55,007 --> 00:00:57,909
to Houston on an old tanker called

25
00:00:57,909 --> 00:00:58,934
the Ideal X.

26
00:00:59,114 --> 00:01:01,206
The world of shipping was never the

27
00:01:01,206 --> 00:01:01,505
same.

28
00:01:01,856 --> 00:01:04,771
As always, every fact comes from a

29
00:01:04,771 --> 00:01:07,428
named source, port authorities,

30
00:01:07,428 --> 00:01:11,544
standards organizations, and maritime histories,

31
00:01:11,544 --> 00:01:14,116
all listed in the description.

32
00:01:14,296 --> 00:01:16,763
Let's start with the strangest thing a

33
00:01:16,763 --> 00:01:17,802
port crane does.

34
00:01:17,982 --> 00:01:20,175
It lifts its own arm into the

35
00:01:20,175 --> 00:01:20,478
sky.

36
00:01:20,828 --> 00:01:23,245
Why do port cranes lift their long

37
00:01:23,245 --> 00:01:25,379
arms straight up into the sky?

38
00:01:25,559 --> 00:01:28,176
Because when a ship arrives or leaves,

39
00:01:28,176 --> 00:01:29,898
the arm, called the boom,

40
00:01:29,898 --> 00:01:31,689
would be right in the way.

41
00:01:31,869 --> 00:01:34,062
The boom sticks out far over the

42
00:01:34,062 --> 00:01:36,188
water to reach across the ship.

43
00:01:36,368 --> 00:01:37,851
A ship's bridge,

44
00:01:37,851 --> 00:01:41,467
its masts and antennas can stand dozens

45
00:01:41,467 --> 00:01:42,858
of meters tall.

46
00:01:43,038 --> 00:01:45,348
So the boom is hinged near the

47
00:01:45,348 --> 00:01:47,812
crane, and big cables pull it up

48
00:01:47,812 --> 00:01:49,891
until it points at the sky,

49
00:01:49,891 --> 00:01:52,586
leaving the berth free for the ship

50
00:01:52,586 --> 00:01:53,510
to slide in.

51
00:01:53,860 --> 00:01:55,753
Once the ship is tied up,

52
00:01:55,753 --> 00:01:58,480
the boom comes back down and becomes

53
00:01:58,480 --> 00:02:00,222
a bridge over the deck.

54
00:02:00,402 --> 00:02:02,901
A small cart called the trolley runs

55
00:02:02,901 --> 00:02:05,747
along it, carrying the container from the

56
00:02:05,747 --> 00:02:06,996
ship to the shore.

57
00:02:07,176 --> 00:02:08,663
On the biggest cranes,

58
00:02:08,663 --> 00:02:11,432
the boom reaches about seventy meters out

59
00:02:11,432 --> 00:02:12,446
over the water,

60
00:02:12,446 --> 00:02:14,270
more than two hundred feet.

61
00:02:14,450 --> 00:02:16,940
That is the length needed to grab

62
00:02:16,940 --> 00:02:19,733
the farthest box on the farthest side

63
00:02:19,733 --> 00:02:21,242
of the widest ships.

64
00:02:21,592 --> 00:02:24,345
When several cranes stand at a quiet

65
00:02:24,345 --> 00:02:26,563
berth with their arms raised,

66
00:02:26,563 --> 00:02:28,782
they look like a row of giant

67
00:02:28,782 --> 00:02:29,393
animals.

68
00:02:29,573 --> 00:02:32,362
In fact, the legend says that the

69
00:02:32,362 --> 00:02:35,489
walkers of Star Wars were inspired by

70
00:02:35,489 --> 00:02:37,433
port cranes in Oakland,

71
00:02:37,433 --> 00:02:38,362
California.

72
00:02:38,542 --> 00:02:41,447
Whether that story is true or not,

73
00:02:41,447 --> 00:02:44,011
the shape has a simple reason.

74
00:02:44,191 --> 00:02:46,061
A crane with its arm up is

75
00:02:46,061 --> 00:02:47,931
a crane that is out of the

76
00:02:47,931 --> 00:02:48,219
way.

77
00:02:48,570 --> 00:02:51,251
Why are container cranes so tall?

78
00:02:51,431 --> 00:02:53,730
Because the ships keep growing.

79
00:02:53,910 --> 00:02:56,845
The biggest container ships today are about

80
00:02:56,845 --> 00:02:59,439
four hundred meters long and more than

81
00:02:59,439 --> 00:03:00,667
sixty meters wide.

82
00:03:00,847 --> 00:03:03,839
They carry boxes stacked up to around

83
00:03:03,839 --> 00:03:06,508
ten high below deck and about ten

84
00:03:06,508 --> 00:03:07,801
high above deck,

85
00:03:07,801 --> 00:03:10,066
and twenty-four rows across.

86
00:03:10,246 --> 00:03:12,354
To lift the top box over the

87
00:03:12,354 --> 00:03:13,635
top of the stack,

88
00:03:13,635 --> 00:03:16,271
the crane needs a lifting height of

89
00:03:16,271 --> 00:03:18,982
around fifty meters above the rails,

90
00:03:18,982 --> 00:03:21,693
as tall as a fifteen-story building.

91
00:03:22,043 --> 00:03:24,019
In the nineteen eighties,

92
00:03:24,019 --> 00:03:27,260
many cranes were built for Panamax ships,

93
00:03:27,260 --> 00:03:30,263
the biggest that could fit through the

94
00:03:30,263 --> 00:03:32,635
old locks of the Panama Canal,

95
00:03:32,635 --> 00:03:35,243
about thirteen containers across.

96
00:03:35,423 --> 00:03:38,140
Then came post-Panamax ships,

97
00:03:38,140 --> 00:03:40,764
and then super post-Panamax.

98
00:03:40,944 --> 00:03:43,420
Many ports had to buy new cranes,

99
00:03:43,420 --> 00:03:46,122
or literally raise their old cranes,

100
00:03:46,122 --> 00:03:49,124
cutting the legs and inserting new steel

101
00:03:49,124 --> 00:03:51,300
sections to make them taller.

102
00:03:51,650 --> 00:03:54,947
The reason ships grow is simple economics.

103
00:03:55,127 --> 00:03:57,916
One huge ship with one crew and

104
00:03:57,916 --> 00:04:01,515
one engine moves boxes more cheaply than

105
00:04:01,515 --> 00:04:03,045
two smaller ones.

106
00:04:03,225 --> 00:04:06,130
So shipping lines order bigger ships,

107
00:04:06,130 --> 00:04:08,957
and every port that wants to receive

108
00:04:08,957 --> 00:04:10,920
them needs bigger cranes,

109
00:04:10,920 --> 00:04:13,511
deeper water, and stronger docks.

110
00:04:13,691 --> 00:04:16,424
The crane is tall because the ship

111
00:04:16,424 --> 00:04:18,594
is big, and the ship is big

112
00:04:18,594 --> 00:04:21,006
because the cheapest box wins.

113
00:04:21,356 --> 00:04:23,895
Why does the crane hold a container

114
00:04:23,895 --> 00:04:26,362
with a flat steel frame instead of

115
00:04:26,362 --> 00:04:26,870
a hook?

116
00:04:27,050 --> 00:04:29,500
That frame is called a spreader.

117
00:04:29,680 --> 00:04:31,929
At each corner it has a pin

118
00:04:31,929 --> 00:04:33,511
called a twistlock.

119
00:04:33,691 --> 00:04:36,269
The spreader lands on top of the

120
00:04:36,269 --> 00:04:38,927
box, the four pins drop into four

121
00:04:38,927 --> 00:04:42,068
holes, and then each pin rotates ninety

122
00:04:42,068 --> 00:04:42,712
degrees.

123
00:04:42,892 --> 00:04:45,657
Its head is now crossways inside the

124
00:04:45,657 --> 00:04:47,885
hole, and it cannot come out.

125
00:04:48,065 --> 00:04:49,992
The box is locked.

126
00:04:50,172 --> 00:04:52,175
No ropes, no hooks,

127
00:04:52,175 --> 00:04:54,073
no workers on top.

128
00:04:54,253 --> 00:04:55,507
It takes seconds.

129
00:04:55,857 --> 00:04:58,643
The spreader is also telescopic.

130
00:04:58,823 --> 00:05:00,118
In a few seconds,

131
00:05:00,118 --> 00:05:02,481
it can stretch from the size of

132
00:05:02,481 --> 00:05:05,681
a twenty-foot container to a forty-foot or

133
00:05:05,681 --> 00:05:07,205
forty-five-foot one.

134
00:05:07,385 --> 00:05:10,949
Many can even lift two twenty-foot boxes

135
00:05:10,949 --> 00:05:13,978
at once, and some cranes carry two

136
00:05:13,978 --> 00:05:16,650
forty-foot boxes side by side.

137
00:05:16,830 --> 00:05:19,948
Sensors tell the operator when all four

138
00:05:19,948 --> 00:05:21,707
twistlocks are seated,

139
00:05:21,707 --> 00:05:24,266
and a lift is blocked until they

140
00:05:24,266 --> 00:05:24,586
are.

141
00:05:24,936 --> 00:05:27,118
That speed is the whole point.

142
00:05:27,298 --> 00:05:29,893
A good crane team can move around

143
00:05:29,893 --> 00:05:32,801
thirty boxes an hour with each crane.

144
00:05:32,981 --> 00:05:36,080
With several cranes working one ship,

145
00:05:36,080 --> 00:05:38,928
thousands of boxes can be moved in

146
00:05:38,928 --> 00:05:40,017
a day or two.

147
00:05:40,197 --> 00:05:42,800
A ship that once stayed a week

148
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:44,796
in port can now arrive,

149
00:05:44,796 --> 00:05:47,834
unload, reload and leave in a short

150
00:05:47,834 --> 00:05:50,958
stay, because of four pins that turn

151
00:05:50,958 --> 00:05:52,260
ninety degrees.

152
00:05:52,610 --> 00:05:55,547
Why does every shipping container in the

153
00:05:55,547 --> 00:05:57,897
world have the same holes at its

154
00:05:57,897 --> 00:05:58,484
corners?

155
00:05:58,664 --> 00:06:01,217
They are called corner castings,

156
00:06:01,217 --> 00:06:04,249
solid steel blocks with oval openings.

157
00:06:04,429 --> 00:06:07,352
They are where the twistlocks grab the

158
00:06:07,352 --> 00:06:10,045
box, where boxes lock to each other

159
00:06:10,045 --> 00:06:10,892
in a stack,

160
00:06:10,892 --> 00:06:13,584
and where they lock onto trucks and

161
00:06:13,584 --> 00:06:14,123
trains.

162
00:06:14,303 --> 00:06:17,070
And their size and position are set

163
00:06:17,070 --> 00:06:19,205
by international standards,

164
00:06:19,205 --> 00:06:21,260
so a box from China fits a

165
00:06:21,260 --> 00:06:23,711
crane in Germany and a truck in

166
00:06:23,711 --> 00:06:24,264
Brazil.

167
00:06:24,615 --> 00:06:26,879
This standard is the real invention.

168
00:06:27,059 --> 00:06:31,169
Malcom McLean's containers worked because they were

169
00:06:31,169 --> 00:06:32,217
all the same,

170
00:06:32,217 --> 00:06:35,441
and in the nineteen sixties the industry

171
00:06:35,441 --> 00:06:37,456
agreed on standard sizes,

172
00:06:37,456 --> 00:06:41,082
now set by the International Organization for

173
00:06:41,082 --> 00:06:42,372
Standardization.

174
00:06:42,552 --> 00:06:45,257
The most common box is twenty or

175
00:06:45,257 --> 00:06:48,299
forty feet long and eight feet wide.

176
00:06:48,479 --> 00:06:51,515
That is why port capacity is measured

177
00:06:51,515 --> 00:06:54,551
in TEU, twenty-foot equivalent units.

178
00:06:54,901 --> 00:06:58,093
Those little corners carry enormous loads.

179
00:06:58,273 --> 00:07:01,292
A loaded container can be stacked under

180
00:07:01,292 --> 00:07:04,001
eight or nine other loaded boxes on

181
00:07:04,001 --> 00:07:06,478
a ship, and all that weight goes

182
00:07:06,478 --> 00:07:08,800
down through the corner posts,

183
00:07:08,800 --> 00:07:09,883
not the walls.

184
00:07:10,063 --> 00:07:12,722
The walls are thin corrugated steel.

185
00:07:12,902 --> 00:07:14,794
The corners are the skeleton.

186
00:07:14,974 --> 00:07:17,688
An empty container weighs only a few

187
00:07:17,688 --> 00:07:20,327
tons, but its four corners can hold

188
00:07:20,327 --> 00:07:20,930
a tower.

189
00:07:21,281 --> 00:07:23,778
Why does the crane operator sit in

190
00:07:23,778 --> 00:07:25,762
a cabin with a glass floor?

191
00:07:25,942 --> 00:07:28,637
Because the operator's job is to look

192
00:07:28,637 --> 00:07:29,657
straight down.

193
00:07:29,837 --> 00:07:32,332
The cabin hangs under the trolley and

194
00:07:32,332 --> 00:07:33,411
travels with it,

195
00:07:33,411 --> 00:07:34,760
high above the ship.

196
00:07:34,940 --> 00:07:37,757
The operator sits leaning forward,

197
00:07:37,757 --> 00:07:41,071
watching the spreader drop into a narrow

198
00:07:41,071 --> 00:07:43,391
slot dozens of meters below,

199
00:07:43,391 --> 00:07:46,457
with only a few centimeters to spare.

200
00:07:46,637 --> 00:07:48,320
Doing that for hours,

201
00:07:48,320 --> 00:07:50,002
in wind and at night,

202
00:07:50,002 --> 00:07:53,127
takes enormous skill and concentration.

203
00:07:53,477 --> 00:07:56,287
The spreader swings like a pendulum when

204
00:07:56,287 --> 00:07:57,552
the trolley stops.

205
00:07:57,732 --> 00:08:01,144
Modern cranes have anti-sway systems that move

206
00:08:01,144 --> 00:08:03,296
the trolley in a smart way to

207
00:08:03,296 --> 00:08:04,557
cancel the swing,

208
00:08:04,557 --> 00:08:07,005
the same idea as moving your hand

209
00:08:07,005 --> 00:08:09,454
under a hanging object to keep it

210
00:08:09,454 --> 00:08:09,899
still.

211
00:08:10,079 --> 00:08:12,993
Still, wind, rain and a moving ship

212
00:08:12,993 --> 00:08:15,907
make every lift a little different.

213
00:08:16,087 --> 00:08:19,242
Crane operators are among the best paid

214
00:08:19,242 --> 00:08:21,021
workers in many ports.

215
00:08:21,372 --> 00:08:22,801
More and more,

216
00:08:22,801 --> 00:08:24,843
that cabin is empty.

217
00:08:25,023 --> 00:08:26,815
In automated terminals,

218
00:08:26,815 --> 00:08:29,776
such as those in Rotterdam and several

219
00:08:29,776 --> 00:08:32,815
Asian ports, the operator works from an

220
00:08:32,815 --> 00:08:33,984
office on land,

221
00:08:33,984 --> 00:08:37,490
watching cameras and helping the machine only

222
00:08:37,490 --> 00:08:40,139
for the hardest parts of the lift.

223
00:08:40,319 --> 00:08:42,740
The view changed from a glass floor

224
00:08:42,740 --> 00:08:43,570
to a screen.

225
00:08:43,750 --> 00:08:45,248
The precision did not.

226
00:08:45,598 --> 00:08:48,175
Why does a port crane stand on

227
00:08:48,175 --> 00:08:49,464
so many wheels?

228
00:08:49,644 --> 00:08:51,619
Because it weighs as much as a

229
00:08:51,619 --> 00:08:53,661
small ship, and it has to move.

230
00:08:53,841 --> 00:08:56,433
The crane rolls along two rails that

231
00:08:56,433 --> 00:08:58,377
run the length of the dock,

232
00:08:58,377 --> 00:09:00,249
so it can line up with any

233
00:09:00,249 --> 00:09:02,049
row of boxes on the ship.

234
00:09:02,229 --> 00:09:04,781
Its weight, plus the load and the

235
00:09:04,781 --> 00:09:06,172
force of the wind,

236
00:09:06,172 --> 00:09:09,265
is spread over many small wheels grouped

237
00:09:09,265 --> 00:09:10,193
at each leg,

238
00:09:10,193 --> 00:09:13,131
because a few large wheels would crush

239
00:09:13,131 --> 00:09:13,827
the dock.

240
00:09:14,178 --> 00:09:15,860
The legs are far apart,

241
00:09:15,860 --> 00:09:17,836
often around thirty meters,

242
00:09:17,836 --> 00:09:20,762
so trucks and vehicles can drive between

243
00:09:20,762 --> 00:09:23,396
them and park right under the crane.

244
00:09:23,576 --> 00:09:26,230
The dock itself is a serious piece

245
00:09:26,230 --> 00:09:29,509
of engineering, often built on deep piles,

246
00:09:29,509 --> 00:09:32,709
because it must carry these moving giants

247
00:09:32,709 --> 00:09:35,910
plus stacks of loaded containers right at

248
00:09:35,910 --> 00:09:37,705
the edge of deep water.

249
00:09:38,056 --> 00:09:41,075
Power usually comes through a thick cable

250
00:09:41,075 --> 00:09:43,505
that unrolls from a giant reel as

251
00:09:43,505 --> 00:09:45,788
the crane moves along the dock.

252
00:09:45,968 --> 00:09:48,754
Many cranes even recover energy.

253
00:09:48,934 --> 00:09:51,036
When a container is lowered,

254
00:09:51,036 --> 00:09:53,287
the motors work as generators,

255
00:09:53,287 --> 00:09:55,764
the same trick as an electric car

256
00:09:55,764 --> 00:09:56,365
braking.

257
00:09:56,545 --> 00:09:59,783
A crane lowering thousands of boxes a

258
00:09:59,783 --> 00:10:02,584
day can feed power back into the

259
00:10:02,584 --> 00:10:03,197
system.

260
00:10:03,547 --> 00:10:06,149
Why do new port cranes often cross

261
00:10:06,149 --> 00:10:08,446
the ocean already standing up?

262
00:10:08,626 --> 00:10:11,501
Because building them in one factory and

263
00:10:11,501 --> 00:10:14,664
shipping them complete is faster and cheaper

264
00:10:14,664 --> 00:10:17,395
than assembling them piece by piece at

265
00:10:17,395 --> 00:10:18,042
the port.

266
00:10:18,222 --> 00:10:21,515
Special heavy-lift ships carry two,

267
00:10:21,515 --> 00:10:25,091
three or more giant cranes standing on

268
00:10:25,091 --> 00:10:27,161
deck, fully assembled.

269
00:10:27,341 --> 00:10:31,304
Most of the world's large ship-to-shore cranes

270
00:10:31,304 --> 00:10:33,888
come from one Chinese company,

271
00:10:33,888 --> 00:10:34,319
ZPMC.

272
00:10:34,669 --> 00:10:36,224
The trip can be tricky.

273
00:10:36,404 --> 00:10:39,276
Cranes arriving in San Francisco Bay have

274
00:10:39,276 --> 00:10:41,378
had to wait for the right tide

275
00:10:41,378 --> 00:10:44,110
and conditions to pass under the Golden

276
00:10:44,110 --> 00:10:46,842
Gate Bridge with very little clearance.

277
00:10:47,022 --> 00:10:48,307
At the destination,

278
00:10:48,307 --> 00:10:50,607
the cranes are rolled off the ship

279
00:10:50,607 --> 00:10:52,095
onto the dock's rails.

280
00:10:52,275 --> 00:10:54,562
A port can go from an empty

281
00:10:54,562 --> 00:10:57,527
berth to working cranes in a matter

282
00:10:57,527 --> 00:10:58,289
of weeks.

283
00:10:58,640 --> 00:11:02,203
That concentration has also become a political

284
00:11:02,203 --> 00:11:02,668
issue.

285
00:11:02,848 --> 00:11:04,971
In two thousand twenty-four,

286
00:11:04,971 --> 00:11:08,306
the United States announced plans to support

287
00:11:08,306 --> 00:11:10,429
crane manufacturing at home,

288
00:11:10,429 --> 00:11:13,689
partly because of concerns about relying on

289
00:11:13,689 --> 00:11:17,024
foreign-made cranes with connected software.

290
00:11:17,204 --> 00:11:20,556
A piece of steel that looks simple

291
00:11:20,556 --> 00:11:23,218
is also a computer on legs.

292
00:11:23,568 --> 00:11:25,809
Why are port cranes chained to the

293
00:11:25,809 --> 00:11:27,260
ground before a storm?

294
00:11:27,440 --> 00:11:30,254
Because a crane on rails is a

295
00:11:30,254 --> 00:11:32,293
giant sail on wheels.

296
00:11:32,473 --> 00:11:34,922
A strong wind can push it along

297
00:11:34,922 --> 00:11:37,372
its rails, or even tip it over.

298
00:11:37,552 --> 00:11:40,640
So every crane has storm anchors.

299
00:11:40,820 --> 00:11:43,626
Heavy pins drop into holes in the

300
00:11:43,626 --> 00:11:46,942
dock, and tie-downs connect the legs to

301
00:11:46,942 --> 00:11:48,982
anchors in the concrete.

302
00:11:49,162 --> 00:11:52,225
An anemometer at the top measures the

303
00:11:52,225 --> 00:11:54,875
wind, and above a certain speed,

304
00:11:54,875 --> 00:11:56,116
work must stop.

305
00:11:56,467 --> 00:11:57,790
This is not theory.

306
00:11:57,970 --> 00:11:59,714
In two thousand three,

307
00:11:59,714 --> 00:12:02,489
Typhoon Maemi hit the port of Busan

308
00:12:02,489 --> 00:12:03,678
in South Korea,

309
00:12:03,678 --> 00:12:06,770
and the wind knocked over several giant

310
00:12:06,770 --> 00:12:08,117
container cranes.

311
00:12:08,297 --> 00:12:10,085
Some fell onto each other.

312
00:12:10,265 --> 00:12:13,312
Cranes have also collapsed in other storms

313
00:12:13,312 --> 00:12:15,633
and in accidents when a ship hit

314
00:12:15,633 --> 00:12:17,301
a crane while berthing.

315
00:12:17,481 --> 00:12:18,828
When a crane falls,

316
00:12:18,828 --> 00:12:20,673
it can take a berth out of

317
00:12:20,673 --> 00:12:22,020
service for months.

318
00:12:22,371 --> 00:12:24,799
That is why the boom position matters

319
00:12:24,799 --> 00:12:25,784
in a storm too.

320
00:12:25,964 --> 00:12:29,037
A raised boom catches the wind differently

321
00:12:29,037 --> 00:12:30,428
than a lowered one,

322
00:12:30,428 --> 00:12:32,842
and port rules say how each crane

323
00:12:32,842 --> 00:12:33,940
must be parked.

324
00:12:34,120 --> 00:12:35,798
Before a hurricane,

325
00:12:35,798 --> 00:12:37,741
crews park the cranes,

326
00:12:37,741 --> 00:12:39,065
lock them down,

327
00:12:39,065 --> 00:12:39,948
and leave.

328
00:12:40,128 --> 00:12:42,470
The safest crane in a storm is

329
00:12:42,470 --> 00:12:44,656
one that cannot move at all.

330
00:12:45,006 --> 00:12:48,001
Why are there vertical steel rails inside

331
00:12:48,001 --> 00:12:49,243
a container ship?

332
00:12:49,423 --> 00:12:51,374
They are called cell guides.

333
00:12:51,554 --> 00:12:54,708
Below deck, the hold is divided into

334
00:12:54,708 --> 00:12:57,688
slots, and each slot has guides at

335
00:12:57,688 --> 00:12:59,178
its four corners,

336
00:12:59,178 --> 00:13:01,631
like a giant elevator shaft.

337
00:13:01,811 --> 00:13:04,692
The crane simply lowers the box into

338
00:13:04,692 --> 00:13:07,253
the slot, and the guides lead it

339
00:13:07,253 --> 00:13:08,533
down into place.

340
00:13:08,713 --> 00:13:11,048
No lashing is needed below deck,

341
00:13:11,048 --> 00:13:13,673
because the guides hold the stack in

342
00:13:13,673 --> 00:13:15,935
place even when the ship rolls.

343
00:13:16,285 --> 00:13:18,665
On deck, there are no guides.

344
00:13:18,845 --> 00:13:21,773
Boxes are locked to each other with

345
00:13:21,773 --> 00:13:25,120
twistlocks and tied down with long steel

346
00:13:25,120 --> 00:13:27,212
rods called lashing bars,

347
00:13:27,212 --> 00:13:29,890
put in by hand by workers called

348
00:13:29,890 --> 00:13:30,559
lashers.

349
00:13:30,739 --> 00:13:32,273
In very bad weather,

350
00:13:32,273 --> 00:13:34,419
these stacks can still fail.

351
00:13:34,599 --> 00:13:35,981
In twenty twenty,

352
00:13:35,981 --> 00:13:38,582
the ship ONE Apus lost more than

353
00:13:38,582 --> 00:13:42,483
eighteen hundred containers overboard in a storm

354
00:13:42,483 --> 00:13:43,702
in the Pacific.

355
00:13:44,052 --> 00:13:46,850
That is why ships plan every box.

356
00:13:47,030 --> 00:13:50,952
A computer decides where each container goes,

357
00:13:50,952 --> 00:13:52,259
heavy ones low,

358
00:13:52,259 --> 00:13:53,654
light ones high,

359
00:13:53,654 --> 00:13:55,572
dangerous cargo apart,

360
00:13:55,572 --> 00:13:58,186
and boxes for the next port on

361
00:13:58,186 --> 00:13:58,535
top.

362
00:13:58,715 --> 00:14:01,722
The crane is not just lifting boxes.

363
00:14:01,902 --> 00:14:04,230
It is solving a giant puzzle,

364
00:14:04,230 --> 00:14:05,756
in the right order,

365
00:14:05,756 --> 00:14:08,325
so the ship stays stable and the

366
00:14:08,325 --> 00:14:10,412
next port can unload fast.

367
00:14:10,763 --> 00:14:14,147
Why do ports use strange machines that

368
00:14:14,147 --> 00:14:16,463
look like boxes on stilts?

369
00:14:16,643 --> 00:14:18,396
They are straddle carriers.

370
00:14:18,576 --> 00:14:20,595
They drive over a container,

371
00:14:20,595 --> 00:14:23,118
lift it up between their tall legs,

372
00:14:23,118 --> 00:14:24,416
and carry it away.

373
00:14:24,596 --> 00:14:26,849
Because they straddle the box,

374
00:14:26,849 --> 00:14:29,402
they can stack containers in rows,

375
00:14:29,402 --> 00:14:30,679
drive over a row,

376
00:14:30,679 --> 00:14:32,932
and pick up the one they need.

377
00:14:33,112 --> 00:14:35,515
They connect the crane at the ship

378
00:14:35,515 --> 00:14:37,353
to the stacks in the yard,

379
00:14:37,353 --> 00:14:37,918
nonstop.

380
00:14:38,269 --> 00:14:40,649
Other ports use a different system.

381
00:14:40,829 --> 00:14:44,230
Driverless vehicles carry boxes from the ship

382
00:14:44,230 --> 00:14:47,405
cranes to automated stacking cranes in the

383
00:14:47,405 --> 00:14:47,783
yard.

384
00:14:47,963 --> 00:14:51,485
The first terminal with automated guided vehicles

385
00:14:51,485 --> 00:14:54,720
opened in Rotterdam in nineteen ninety-three.

386
00:14:54,900 --> 00:14:57,973
Today, many of the busiest terminals run

387
00:14:57,973 --> 00:15:00,584
day and night with very few people

388
00:15:00,584 --> 00:15:01,506
in the yard.

389
00:15:01,856 --> 00:15:04,096
Every system has the same goal.

390
00:15:04,276 --> 00:15:06,915
Keep the giant crane at the ship

391
00:15:06,915 --> 00:15:08,400
busy every second,

392
00:15:08,400 --> 00:15:11,534
because the ship is the most expensive

393
00:15:11,534 --> 00:15:13,019
thing in the port.

394
00:15:13,199 --> 00:15:15,949
A ship waiting in port costs a

395
00:15:15,949 --> 00:15:17,599
fortune every day.

396
00:15:17,779 --> 00:15:20,125
So everything in the terminal,

397
00:15:20,125 --> 00:15:22,940
from straddle carriers to computers,

398
00:15:22,940 --> 00:15:24,896
exists to feed the crane.

399
00:15:25,246 --> 00:15:26,732
So here are the ten.

400
00:15:26,912 --> 00:15:28,542
An arm that folds up out of

401
00:15:28,542 --> 00:15:29,025
the way.

402
00:15:29,205 --> 00:15:31,817
A height set by the biggest ships.

403
00:15:31,997 --> 00:15:34,600
A spreader with four turning pins,

404
00:15:34,600 --> 00:15:37,433
and corner holes that fit every crane

405
00:15:37,433 --> 00:15:38,429
in the world.

406
00:15:38,609 --> 00:15:40,889
A cabin with a glass floor,

407
00:15:40,889 --> 00:15:43,423
wheels that spread the weight,

408
00:15:43,423 --> 00:15:46,801
cranes that cross the ocean standing up,

409
00:15:46,801 --> 00:15:48,997
anchors against the storm,

410
00:15:48,997 --> 00:15:50,855
rails inside the ship,

411
00:15:50,855 --> 00:15:53,643
and machines on stilts to feed it

412
00:15:53,643 --> 00:15:53,980
all.

413
00:15:54,331 --> 00:15:56,938
The next time you open a package,

414
00:15:56,938 --> 00:15:59,783
remember it probably rode in a steel

415
00:15:59,783 --> 00:16:02,074
box, under a crane like this.

416
00:16:02,254 --> 00:16:05,327
If you want more machines explained part

417
00:16:05,327 --> 00:16:08,245
by part, subscribe to Machine Reverse.

418
00:16:08,425 --> 00:16:10,465
And tell us in the comments which

419
00:16:10,465 --> 00:16:12,505
machine you want us to take apart

420
00:16:12,505 --> 00:16:12,814
next.
